<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#chu-nom">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chữ Nôm</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nôm, Chu Nom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chữ Nôm is a script for writing the Vietnamese language, built from Chinese characters and new characters formed on their model. Used from about the 13th century until the early 20th, it carried a rich vernacular literature, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-du">Nguyễn Du</a>'s Tale of Kiều, alongside official writing in classical Chinese. It was replaced by the Latin-based <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnamese-alphabet">Vietnamese alphabet</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chu-nom.jpg" alt="Chữ Nôm" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lệ Xuân · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ch%E1%BB%AF_N%C3%B4m_(Kh%E1%BA%A3i_th%C6%B0).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
